        "id": "0b1e4f71-d9bf-47db-ae56-e3982dce3e74",
        "name": "AI in the Audit Workflow",
        "mini_description": "Why audit has a workflow problem, where AI belongs, and how Agentive helps auditors reduce repetitive testing.",
        "order": 1,
        "duration_seconds": 336,
        "total_quiz_questions": 2,
        "horizontal_thumbnail_url": "https://milesone-backend-assets.s3.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/MilesOne/Masterclass/Chapter_Thumbnails/Horizontal/agentive1.jpg",
        "vertical_thumbnail_url": null,
        "square_thumbnail_url": null,
        "hls_video_url": "https://d2eoseju8m2c1y.cloudfront.net/videos/Daniel_Alberson_Agentive_Audit_Automation_1_AI_in_the_Audit_Workflow_Daniel_Alberson/Daniel_Alberson_Agentive_Audit_Automation_1_AI_in_the_Audit_Workflow_Daniel_Alberson.m3u8",
        "transcript_text": "<div style=\"font-family: Arial, sans-serif; background-color: #0d1a2d; color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; padding: 20px; margin: 10px auto; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); max-width: 800px; line-height: 1.6;\">         <div style=\"margin: 15px 0; color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6;\"><h3 style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 24px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.2;;\"><strong style=\"color: #ffffff;;\">Episode 1 - AI in the Audit Workflow</strong><br></h3><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Welcome. I'm Daniel Alberson, co-founder and CEO of Agentive. If you've ever chased down client documents at 11:00 PM, explained to a first-year how to trace and vouch for the fifth time, or watched a client dump 200 files into a folder with no naming convention, you know the audit has a workflow problem. That's what this masterclass is about.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Not \"AI is the future.\" You already know that. This is about getting specific. Where does AI actually belong in an audit workflow? What should never change? And what does it look like when AI is purpose-built for audit? Here's what you'll see today. One, a live demo of nonprofit grant revenue testing. I'll show you how one firm went from eight hours to an hour and a half on this exact workflow. Two, I'll show you a live demo of expense testing mapped to assertions: accuracy, cutoff, and classification. A firm using this approach cut government contractor expense compliance testing from 10 hours to 2. Third, I'll show you a live demo of our financial statement review and footnotes review product. It's structured, repeatable, and citation-based. We have many firms that use this product and find issues in previously filed public financial statements when they're doing their first demo. And across firms, we consistently see 30% time savings spent on repetitive testing. If any of that sounds relevant to your world, stay with me. If it resonates at the end, I'll tell you exactly how to try it yourself. Let's go.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Growing up, some of the people I admired most were accountants: partners at firms, leaders in their communities, with wonderful families, stable, trusted, and smart. I wanted that life, so I studied accounting at Brigham Young University. I joined Ernst &amp; Young as an auditor, and pretty quickly, like in my first few months, I ran into a reality a lot of people don't say out loud. The bulk of early audit work is repetitive and requires little judgment. You pull the sample, you request support, you open PDFs, you trace and vouch, and you document conclusions over and over and over again. And it's important work, and it has to be done right. But the procedure itself, it's just that: it's procedural. It's template-driven. It's exactly where burnout happens. I saw talented people\u2014smart, curious people who could have been solving real problems\u2014spending 80% of their time on mechanical execution. And the message when you try to change it is, \"Do it the way we tell you. Get fast. Earn the right to think later.\" I get the intent. I don't blame anyone for it, but I had a strong reaction: I can't live like this. Not because I'm lazy, but because I care too much about fixing broken systems to spend my career inside of one without trying to change it.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">So I left. I transitioned internally at EY to their technology consulting practice. I got into Northwestern's master's in product design and engineering program. I started learning how the best software in the world gets built, not from accountants, but from engineers and designers at the best companies in the world. And then I dropped out because Facebook recruited me to be a product manager on their augmented reality glasses team. It's the literal frontier of computing. It's what I thought my dream was. So I went from tracing invoices and workpapers to doing technology consulting to building the future at one of the biggest technology companies. And here's the thing that changed me. At Meta, the instinct when something is repetitive and procedural is, \"Why is a human doing this? How can we improve it?\" This question is embedded in the culture, in the engineering process, and in every product review. And I realized nobody's asking the question about audit. We've normalized the grind. We call it paying your dues. We staff it with the youngest, least experienced people and wonder why retention is a crisis.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">So I left Meta and came back to audit, not because I missed the workpapers, but because I finally had the language and tools to fix the problems I ran from. So I started Agentive with my co-founders. Nathan Turnbow came from Imagine Learning's AI engineering leadership team, and before that, Dell, where he worked on data security and privacy products. And Jason Draper joined us from a startup where he was the CTO, and before that, he was in emergency war planning for the U.S. Air Force. Between the three of us, we cover the full stack: audit experience, product design, and AI engineering. We went through Y Combinator, the same program behind Airbnb, Stripe, and Dropbox. We're backed by Foundation Capital, Dell Technologies, and, this is the part that matters most to you, investors who come from inside the public accounting profession. And the reason I'm telling you all this isn't to name-drop. It's because I want you to know that this isn't a tech bro with a ChatGPT wrapper telling auditors how to do their jobs. This is a team that started in audit, lived the pain, left and learned how to build great software, and came back because we believe auditors deserve better tools.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">And there's a personal reason I still care. I have family members entering audit right now. I have friends who dread the audit every year as clients. I've seen what happens to firms that can't retain staff. I've seen what happens to clients who lose their auditor and scramble to find a new one. This isn't abstract for me. I want auditors to enjoy auditing. I want clients to stop dreading the audit. That's the mission. That's why Agentive exists. That's why we've built what I'm going to show you today. All of these exciting demos are coming to you in future episodes. I'll see you there.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\"><br><br></p></div>   <div style=\"font-size: 14px; margin-top: 20px; text-align: center; color: #ffffff; opacity: 0.8; width: 100%;\"> <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; gap: 0; text-align: center; margin: 20px 0;\">   <hr style=\"flex: 1; border: 0; height: 1px; background: #ffffff; width: calc(100% - 150px);\" />   <img src=\"https://storage.googleapis.com/masterclass-bucket-usa2/media/web-app/miles-masterclass.logo.webp\" alt=\"Miles Masterclass Logo\" style=\"width: 150px; height: auto; margin: 0;\" /> </div></div> </div>",
