        "hls_video_url": "https://d2eoseju8m2c1y.cloudfront.net/videos/Daniel_Alberson_Agentive_Audit_Automation_2_AI_Agents_Not_Chatbots_for_Audit_Daniel_Alberson/Daniel_Alberson_Agentive_Audit_Automation_2_AI_Agents_Not_Chatbots_for_Audit_Daniel_Alberson.m3u8",
        "transcript_text": "<div style=\"font-family: Arial, sans-serif; background-color: #0d1a2d; color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; padding: 20px; margin: 10px auto; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); max-width: 800px; line-height: 1.6;\">         <div style=\"margin: 15px 0; color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6;\"><h3 style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 24px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.2;;\"><strong style=\"color: #ffffff;;\">Episode 2 - AI Agents, Not Chatbots, for Audit</strong></h3><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">This shift is about agentic workflows, not chatbots. Let's talk about where the pain actually is and where AI fits in. In most firms, especially outside of the top 10, you're operating with some combination of a core audit platform, Excel workpapers, PDFs, a client portal, and a lot of manual interpretation. And the highest-volume pain is the same everywhere: test of details execution and evidence gathering. That creates three problems that feed each other.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">One, burnout and retention. The AICPA has been sounding the alarm for years. The pipeline of new auditors is shrinking, and the people who do enter the profession are leaving faster than they used to. Why? Because the first two to three years feel like data entry with a CPA requirement. That's not a people problem. It's a tooling problem. Two, client friction. Your clients don't enjoy being audited. They especially don't enjoy getting a request list that looks like it was generated in 2008, uploading documents into a black hole, and then getting follow-up questions three weeks later because something didn't match. The client experience in most audits is bad, and it doesn't have to be. Three, engagement economics. A huge amount of billable time gets spent on low-judgment execution. That means either the firm eats the cost or the client pays for hours that don't feel like they're adding value. Neither option is great, and as firms grow, this problem scales linearly: more engagements, more staff hours, more mechanical work.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Now, here's the most important boundary, and I need you to hear this clearly. AI does not replace professional judgment. AI does not set risk levels. It doesn't determine materiality. AI doesn't decide what's sufficient and appropriate. AI does not sign the opinion. This stays human, period, full stop. If anyone tells you otherwise, they're either confused or selling you something you shouldn't buy. Auditors matter.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">So what does AI do? AI executes the procedures and prepares them for human judgment. Think about it this way. By the time you get to test of details, you've already done the hard thinking. You've assessed the risk. You've designed the audit approach. You've determined the appropriate assertions. You've selected the sample. You've defined the procedure. What's left is execution and judgment: open the document, extract the relevant data, compare it to the general ledger, check the dates, check the amounts, check the names, flag exceptions, and document the results. This can now be executed by software because AI models got dramatically better at multi-step tool use.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">AI drafts judgment, but you review its logic, recommendations, and source citations, and then conclude on the judgment yourself. That's the key shift I want you to understand. Most people's mental model of AI is a chatbot. You ask a question, you get an answer, you move on. Turn-based.Audit isn't one question. It's a chain. What account am I testing? What assertions am I testing? What evidence do I need? Does it agree to the general ledger? What terms matter in this contract? Is it in the right period? Is it properly classified? What exceptions exist? How do I document it? That chain, that entire sequence, is now something an AI agent can execute, not by guessing, but by following the procedures you defined on the evidence your client provided and returning results with citations back to source documents. That's the difference between AI as a chatbot and AI as an agentic workflow engine, and that's the inflection point we're in right now.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Two years ago, this wasn't possible. Models weren't good enough. They hallucinated, they couldn't follow multi-step instructions, and they would just give up. They weren't reliable. They couldn't handle real documents with messy formatting. Today they can, and they can do it reliably enough that firms are using it in production on real engagements with real clients, and it's creating real value. And what I'm about to show you is exactly what that looks like.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Let me be really clear about what we are and what we're not. We are not your audit methodology. We're not trying to replace your planning tool, your risk assessment framework, your binder system, or your core audit platform. You use CaseWare, Teammate, or ProSystem fx, or whatever else, we're not competing with that. You still plan the audit where you plan it today. Agentive steps in once you've already decided what you're testing, the assertions you're focused on, and the procedures you plan to perform. Then we connect three things. One, your population, your general ledger, subledger, trial balance, whatever you're sampling from. Two, your request list, structured, selection-linked, and designed so your client actually understands what they need to provide and why. And three, your workpaper logic, the procedures that run automatically when evidence is uploaded and return results with citations back to source documents.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">And the key behavior that changes everything is this: when the client uploads evidence, the test runs automatically in seconds, with citations. Not, \"AI summarized something and you hope it's right.\" It ran the procedure. It extracted the data points. It compared them. It flagged exceptions. And it shows you exactly where in the document it found its answers. Then it's ready for your judgment and conclusion. That's what you're about to see. Three live demos, real workflows, real documents. Let's start with revenue in the next episode.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\"><br></p></div>   <div style=\"font-size: 14px; margin-top: 20px; text-align: center; color: #ffffff; opacity: 0.8; width: 100%;\"> <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; gap: 0; text-align: center; margin: 20px 0;\">   <hr style=\"flex: 1; border: 0; height: 1px; background: #ffffff; width: calc(100% - 150px);\" />   <img src=\"https://storage.googleapis.com/masterclass-bucket-usa2/media/web-app/miles-masterclass.logo.webp\" alt=\"Miles Masterclass Logo\" style=\"width: 150px; height: auto; margin: 0;\" /> </div></div> </div>",
