        "hls_video_url": "https://d2eoseju8m2c1y.cloudfront.net/videos/Daniel_Alberson_Agentive_Audit_Automation_3_Grant_Revenue_Testing_in_Agentive_Daniel_Alberson/Daniel_Alberson_Agentive_Audit_Automation_3_Grant_Revenue_Testing_in_Agentive_Daniel_Alberson.m3u8",
        "transcript_text": "<div style=\"font-family: Arial, sans-serif; background-color: #0d1a2d; color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; padding: 20px; margin: 10px auto; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); max-width: 800px; line-height: 1.6;\">         <div style=\"margin: 15px 0; color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6;\"><h3 style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 24px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.2;;\"><strong style=\"color: #ffffff;;\">Episode 3 - Grant Revenue Testing in Agentive</strong></h3><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Thanks for joining me. Let me walk you through how to do nonprofit grant revenue testing in Agentive. So what I'm showing you right now in Agentive is our engagement view. This is a request list that also serves as the place for you to automate your audit procedure. Clients just see the request list. We have categories like revenue contributions, expense liabilities, payroll, things you're very familiar with that are client-facing. We have recent activity showing you very quickly what's been done on this engagement and by whom. This is a lot of work between me and my colleague Chris, and we have 12 requests total here, and we show what status they're currently in.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">What we're going to do now is create a new request and associate it to a workflow that will help us with this testing. So I'm going to click Create here, and I'm going to start searching. We have a lot of options, different tags. We can start from scratch or roll forward an existing engagement. There's quite a bit we can do here, but I'm just going to start by typing in grant revenue testing, and I'm going to choose this template here that corresponds to ASC 958. As we can see, this grant revenue testing template is to obtain sufficient appropriate audit evidence over the occurrence, accuracy, cutoff, classification, and timing of grant and contribution revenue in accordance with ASC 958. This template will set you up to request grant agreements based on selections, bank statements, and conditional evidence that will help do this audit procedure test.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">I'm going to click Use This Template now. We can create these from scratch, but I'm going to show you these demos, and we'll edit them together. This is what the request looks like at first. You'll notice here it says no table data pasted yet. I am going to select this and click Add Table Data. This is really just adding the selection. So I'm going to come to my files here and open up my selections. Very simply, I'm going to take the column headers and each transaction and copy this, come back to Agentive, and I'm going to paste these values. So now we have these selections. We could do sampling here. We're not going to do that here today, but we could do monetary unit sampling, random sampling, or even journal entry testing. But we're going to click Save This Data. And now we can see that our request list clearly shows what selections we're requesting evidence for and what type of evidence needs to be uploaded for each one.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">So we have grant agreements, conditional evidence, and bank statements that need to be provided for each selection. Clients can upload these one by one, or they can do this in what we like to call a bulk upload, and we'll automatically match this for you based on your logic. I'm going to upload this evidence really quickly, and as you can see, I just have all the bank statements uploaded together, all the conditional evidence formatted together. I'm going to now take the grant agreements and upload them to bulk upload, and we'll see that this is being processed. And while we're doing this, we actually OCR all of these documents and understand very clearly what all of the content is inside each document so that we can programmatically work with it.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Now I'm going to take the conditional evidence, and then I'm going to take the bank statements and upload those. And while that's being processed, I am going to walk you through this workflow. So in Agentive, each request can have a workflow associated with it. So, for example, with these grant agreements, the first thing we're going to do is actually match them back to the grant revenue selections. And we're using our agent to do this matching, so this happens automatically without any input from us directly.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Now we're adding custom columns to our workpaper, and I'll show you what this looks like right now. So we have the match result, which will come back with a check mark or an X mark for whether it was passing or failing. Then we have different testing attributes. We have a short description, and we have columns that collect context for us to understand this audit. So this is determining whether it's a nonreciprocal transfer based on ASC 958. In this specific section here, it defines a contribution as an unconditional transfer of cash or other assets to an NFP, or a settlement or cancellation of its liabilities. It goes into pretty good detail about what's being tested here, and it tells it to respond only with the letter Y for attribute satisfied, N for attribute not satisfied, or NA for not applicable.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">And we do this for all the important criteria for grant revenue testing. Does it have conditions? Are there donor-imposed restrictions? Are there unconditional promises? We can add these very easily ourselves by typing them out. And then we have our testing attributes, like, is this grant contribution supported? Let's actually relabel this one really quickly so it's easy to reference. The grant contribution amount recorded is supported by a grant award letter or agreement. And then that's the audit procedure that will show in your documentation, which we'll show you in a minute. And this is the prompt: Is the grant revenue selection amount recorded supported by a grant award letter agreement? So that is the question that we're having AI analyze as it looks at the specific selection amount and at the documents attributed to it.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">We can actually add more values here that change dynamically based on which selection is being tested. So we can add another AI prompt, add a formula, or add a testing attribute. And we have other features here, like deep analysis and high evaluation, but we'll move past those for now just to show you this. So this is one part of the workflow. It's all the questions we want to ask of the grant agreement and what tests can be done based on the grant agreement alone.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Then we have conditional evidence, which matches back to the grant agreement, and AI will review and figure out how to do that, and it'll show us how it matched after we run this workflow. For our custom columns, we want to know the date the conditions were satisfied. We want to know whether the conditional revenue recognition should take place. I'm going to rename this as Label Two and label this as Three, and I'll show you how we look at this. But testing attribute three, which, yes, one was in the other document group for grant agreements, and two and three are in this conditional evidence, is a simple question like, \"Do the uploaded supporting documents demonstrate that the grant agreement condition was satisfied?\" And the grant condition comes from the other document group for grant agreements.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">So we see this workflow is built linearly, starting with selections, going to grant agreements, going to conditional evidence, and then ending up at bank statements and looking at the matched deposit date. We're having it return the date in a specific format, tracing out to the bank statement, asking whether the grant can be traced back to the bank statement. And we use this prompt here. It's very easy to write these in natural language. We'll show you what this looks like as we run it.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">So now, as we have this, I'm going to come over here to this workflow, and I'm going to click Run. When we run this workflow, it will start running the match for the grant agreements, run custom columns for the grant agreement, which are context that's used when evaluating the testing attributes, and then it will run the match for conditional evidence because some of the matching for conditional evidence relies on the information we'll derive from the grant agreements. And it will keep proceeding through the workflow. So now you can start seeing things being populated. This is going to update in real time, and everything will come back completed after it's done being processed.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">So now the workflow's finished after about a minute or two, depending on the complexity of the test being done. We can click the workpaper column, and we can start reviewing what was done. As you remember, we see the matches come back. These all match, so it means that these selections match to each one of these file names, or these documents. So this is the grant agreement letter that matches to this selection. You'll see these grouped based on these numbers here to the left, but we'll collapse them so they appear together. This matching took place based on the context of what was provided in the selection and the file name.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Now we can see that this description was provided for this grant agreement, and here are the citations that it used to generate this description. Every answer we have provides reasoning on how it executed on its instructions. So we're going to come here and look at this grant agreement. Let's start with nonreciprocal transfer. This question is, are there any nonreciprocal transfers in this document? The reasoning here is saying the document titled grant agreement explicitly states the foundation has awarded a grant of $56,285.87 and that these funds are a restricted grant for a specified purpose, with payment by wire. There's no language indicating the donor will receive goods or services. So under ASC 958, this meets the definition of an unconditional, voluntary, nonreciprocal transfer contribution. So an auditor can look at this, say, \"I agree, let's validate that,\" and record their judgment on that detail.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Now we can come and look at the condition for the same agreement, which is for Bryant Inc., and you'll see that the details in the selection tie back to that. Are there conditions placed on this? This is saying that, yes, there's a donor restriction on use, but no performance barrier, no provision for return of assets, and no right of release for the donor's obligations. So it says under ASC 958, this meets the definition of an unconditional contribution with a donor restriction, not a conditional one. So I can review that and say it's good.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">But let's come down here and jump to one that is conditional. So we've run this workflow for this test, and now we're looking at the grant agreements, specifically the ASC 958 criteria that we need to evaluate. We've said that yes, this is a nonreciprocal transfer based on ASC 958 criteria. Here's the reasoning. I've reviewed that and clicked Validate to record my judgment. Now I'm moving on to whether it's conditional. This one says it's unconditional, and it justifies its reasoning. I can agree with that, or I can click to edit it if it changes.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">I'm going to come down to this next selection that has a condition, and I'm going to click here. We can review the prompt that was written and look at the reasoning here. This agreement is explicitly labeled as a conditional grant. So if we look here at its citation, we see this line says, \"Conditional grant revenue recognition is contingent upon organization must raise matching funds of $27,710.11.\" And here's the reasoning for why it marked this as having a condition. So you can follow this reasoning and validate that through your own use case. We can do the same with donor-imposed restrictions, seeing how this is analyzed and whether it has any unconditional promises. And as we review these details, we'll sit here and record our judgment. The goal is not, once again, to replace auditors. The goal is to help them jump straight to review and point them to source citations that help auditors understand where this context came from so that they can provide their own professional judgment.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">When we leave the grant agreement, we're now going to go over to the conditional evidence and look at how this is related. Only some of these had conditional evidence. Not all of them did. But coming over to conditional evidence, we can see that for the transaction that had a condition, this was matched because the file aligns with the selection's conditional matching requirement, and the date is satisfactory. It goes through these details and explains that it has a match required of 27,710, so it matches the details that were collected in the grant agreement. And now we can look at this date for when the condition was satisfied. Then we can look at these testing attributes.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">So this says, is the grant revenue selection date received recorded on the condition satisfaction date? And this is saying the contribution schedule contains an entry date of February 2, 2025, and the bank deposit slip is the corresponding check and includes the amount in that net deposit total, which corroborates that a date received was recorded on February 3. This document also records the condition as met and revenue recognized, indicating the match was satisfied with the included donations. So we can look through this to confirm the details, but this is how it determined whether the conditional revenue recognition was met. And so the tick mark for this shows that it agreed without exception, as you see here in this pane area.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Let's jump down and look at one that has a tick mark exception. So for this attribute, there was a condition, and this is the conditional support. Here we have a tick mark that was raised by our system saying that this doesn't appear to be agreed without exception. So let's click on this and review its work and determine whether or not we need additional support here, or whether it was recorded correctly.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">So the reason is looking at when the conditional revenue was recognized and whether it was recorded on the right date. So we have the date that it was recorded on. This is from what we call a prompt injection, but you can add reference columns. So this is from the general ledger for this. The date is March 8. And coming here, it's asking, is that on the exact same condition satisfaction date as found in this document, which is the conditional evidence document? This is saying no. The deposit slips shown in the documents are dated February 22, and the schedule of matching contributions lists through March 7, when they finally met those requirements. So this is saying that the entry on March 8 is off because it was actually met on March 7. So we can see that this is just off by a day. It looks like they met the requirements on the 7th, but they recognized revenue on the 8th.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">This would be more of an issue if that was at a quarter-end cutoff or year-end cutoff, but this is close enough. It doesn't materially affect the financial statement. So we're going to click Validate, but we are going to follow up with the client and discuss with them what their controls are, making sure that they recognize revenue on the right date. But this is a minor issue. I might flag that as an exception to follow up with the client and ask why this was recorded as revenue a day late compared to when they met the requirements. We'll flag that for review and talk about it with audit leadership and with the client. So that tick mark appears to be accurate, saying that they recognized revenue a day late, but we could also flag it and change the tick mark to passing without exception very easily.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">So now we're going to take a look at the bank statements and see how this information tied out. We have these match results. Now let's have it trace back to the bank statement. We're asking, does the grant agreement donor name on the amount of 125,000 appear on the bank statement as a singular deposit? This must show both the deposit amount and the donor name to be considered a pass. And this is coming here and saying that yes, it does include both a donor name and the exact amount, 125,000. So we can see that amount right here, and that satisfies that requirement.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">So what we can do is take a workflow of an audit procedure, break it out into small steps that AI needs to gather context for, and then define a testing attribute referencing the context that is used to make that conclusion. AI will draft the initial conclusion, but then you'll come back and provide your judgment, as simple as pressing Validate or Flag as Exception, and record your judgment. You can change these values easily, add comments, and really collaborate with your team here so that you can quickly work your way through audit procedures that used to take you 10 hours, and now you can get them done in an hour and a half with Agentive.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">And in one click, you can export this to Excel with all the files that were provided by the client so that you can store it in your audit binder and have your workgroup review it. I'm going to really quickly export this and show you what this looks like in Agentive. So I just now opened this file, and this is actually what my export looks like. It gives you a place to store your purpose and your scope. It has the source as who provided these files and when they were provided. And then these are the procedures that we did. We traced the bank statements. Conditional revenue recognition showed when conditions were satisfied, and we have a record of when the grant contributions were supported. You see the definitions, we have our tick mark legend as we showed, and we have all of this output into Excel for easy and simple review. It helps you streamline your audit testing and your documentation.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Thank you for going through this test with us. We'd love to talk about how this can benefit you and answer any questions you have, but we can build this out to support your workflow. So now you've seen what Agentive can do for revenue testing, and you can see how this can apply to many of your audit procedures. Let's jump and see what this can do on expenses next.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\"><br></p></div>   <div style=\"font-size: 14px; margin-top: 20px; text-align: center; color: #ffffff; opacity: 0.8; width: 100%;\"> <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; gap: 0; text-align: center; margin: 20px 0;\">   <hr style=\"flex: 1; border: 0; height: 1px; background: #ffffff; width: calc(100% - 150px);\" />   <img src=\"https://storage.googleapis.com/masterclass-bucket-usa2/media/web-app/miles-masterclass.logo.webp\" alt=\"Miles Masterclass Logo\" style=\"width: 150px; height: auto; margin: 0;\" /> </div></div> </div>",
