        "hls_video_url": "https://d2eoseju8m2c1y.cloudfront.net/videos/Daniel_Alberson_Agentive_Audit_Automation_4_Expense_Testing_in_Agentive_Daniel_Alberson/Daniel_Alberson_Agentive_Audit_Automation_4_Expense_Testing_in_Agentive_Daniel_Alberson.m3u8",
        "transcript_text": "<div style=\"font-family: Arial, sans-serif; background-color: #0d1a2d; color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; padding: 20px; margin: 10px auto; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); max-width: 800px; line-height: 1.6;\">         <div style=\"margin: 15px 0; color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6;\"><h3 style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 24px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.2;;\"><strong style=\"color: #ffffff;;\">Episode 4 - Expense Testing in Agentive</strong></h3><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Welcome again. I know we just looked at revenue, but now let's look at expenses and how we can drastically simplify this important but mechanical work so that you can get to judgment. Coming back to Agentive, we see the request list page. I'm going to now create another request from our template. I have the template pulled up here, operating expense. So I'm going to select this. Its description is to test whether sampled operating expenses are valid, accurate, recorded, properly classified and in the right period. We're going to start with our samples. We have these required column headers, but you can map these columns if they are different for one of your clients. You can map them to the required column headers. And we have our invoices, plus testing attributes for those invoices, and a bank statement where we have matching configured.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">So I'm going to click Use This Template, and then I'm going to get started by adding our selection data. Now our request, or our automation, has the selections added in. Now we need to add the invoices and the bank statements, which will follow these processes. So we have custom columns for expected journal entry amount. Now we're asking, what is the total amount of charges related to the expense sample general ledger account? And that's referencing this column here in each selection so that it's unique, and it's being asked of each document based on the context of its selection. What is the total invoice amount? What is the vendor name on the invoice? What is the invoice date? These are things that help us tie out to the bank statement.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">And now we have the accuracy testing attribute, where we test whether the amount in the invoice ties out to the amount that we expected in the journal entry. Then we have our classification prompt and our cutoff prompt that will help us do these testing attributes. But let's just cut to the chase and upload these documents, then run them and start the review. Once again, I have these, and I can just drag and drop the invoices right here in this bulk upload, and then I can drag and drop the bank statements right after that, and this process will kick off and complete this test. Right now it's recognizing the text in every document, structuring it well, and getting it ready to run the workflow. So you'll see this happen very quickly, and it's running the match now and starting off the workflow. We'll show you what that looks like in a second. All you do is click here, and we'll be taken right to it.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Now the workflow is completed, and we have our workpaper. We can start a review. We have our 10 selections, and we have the invoices that have automatically been matched. So if we look at this, this is saying it's matching the invoice to the expense sample, and the reasons why it matched are the vendor, merchant, and receipt contact, which include the name. See how it ties back out to the Starbucks purchase? It matches on the receipt check number, it has the right transaction date, and it has the right total amount. So it has these values that you can tie back to see the exact citations.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Now, the expected journal entry amount. When we ask the prompt, \"What is the total amount of charges related to the GL account from meals and entertainment?\" it looks at this and says the total amount for GL account $67,000 is the total payment recorded on the receipt payment line. That payment amount equals the subtotal plus tax and comes out to $20.59. Notice that we didn't tell it what was in the selection. We asked it blindly, based on the GL, what the expected journal entry amount should be, and then we return the invoice total, compare it to what the amount should be, and do the analysis.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">So now, if we look at one, one is empty. If we look here, this is for Microsoft web hosting, and this says, \"I can't determine the total amount of charges related to web hosting because the invoice does not include the GL account codes or map the line items here.\" So it doesn't understand. AI doesn't understand how to tie this back out to web hosting. But I can take a look here and see this is $135,000, and I, based on my auditor experience, know that all of these Azure details are web hosting. So I can see that this matches. I'm actually just going to take our little citation tool, and I'm going to come over that total and populate that total there. Real quick, I'm going to come back, validate this, validate the one that I just gathered, and now I've made the citation tie back. It ties back out to the invoice total.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Let's see what this is. So this is the same thing, saying it doesn't understand how web hosting works, but if we look at that amount, it's the same amount. I can come here and just bring that value in. Very easy to do, to work with. I can validate it, keep going, and validate the invoice date as it comes through. So this is a hotel portfolio, and it doesn't have an invoice date. So let's see. The March date is the 23rd. I can either type that in, or I can use that tool to grab this value as well. Totally up to you how you want to process that.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Let's take a look at the testing attributes that we set up. The first testing attribute is accuracy. If we look at that, I'm going to come here and click on it. So accuracy is testing whether the expected journal entry amount matches what was actually in the invoice. If we come look at this, this is saying that these amounts equal zero, and so that checks out really well. We can do that for all of these, but that checks out. Those equal each other.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Coming to number two, this is asking whether or not the amount calculated in the invoice journal and expected amount relates to goods or services that are consistent in nature to the expense account for GL account $67,000 - meals and entertainment. This is about classification. So if we look at this, we're seeing if this is classified correctly based on the meals and entertainment general ledger account, and this is consistent with meals and entertainment. I agree and mark that as consistent.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">And let's look at our last testing attribute, which is cutoff, and see if this is done correctly. So the fiscal year is the same as the calendar year. Is the date of goods or services provided in the same year as the expense sample journal entry date, January 10, 2024? And this was done, and it looks like it matches the same year. So that checks out. So cutoff is correct. We can repeat this process, but let's come and look at these tick marks really quickly.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">So for classification, it's asking, does the amount calculated in the invoice's expected journal entry amount relate to goods or services that are consistent with the expense nature of software expenses? Looking at this question, is this related to software expenses? And it's saying advanced sales methodology training, sales performance coaching, and sales analytics, which are not software expenses. I agree. This doesn't look like software expenses. So we're going to flag this as an exception and review this with management.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Let's take a look at the other one. Auditors should look at all of these and conclude on them individually, but once again, we take them straight to context and where they can apply their judgment. The goal is not to avoid having auditors do the judgment. That's not the case at all. The goal is to present them with the information needed to perform the judgment and record their conclusion. And so let's look at B, because I think that may be interesting for us.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">This is saying the fiscal year is the same as the calendar year. Was it done in the same year as the journal entry date? And this shows that the date of travel is actually in 2025, which is not the same timeframe as the journal entry date. So it's flagging this as not in the right cutoff, or not in the same year. So let's flag that as well to review it with our leadership. And if we look at these invoices, we actually take reasons for why things should match and place them here, and then counter-evidence is reasons why information shouldn't match. So in our match results, it's clearly the same transaction based on the amount, the vendor, and those details, but the date clearly is off.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">If we look at this, it is saying the transaction date on the statement, 9/3/2024, is not aligned. The invoice date may indicate the invoice date in the selection is inaccurate or prior-period activity. So it's really important to look at these things when there's contra-evidence or when there are tick marks that are relevant, because those are the highest-risk items. But you should look at every single document and record your judgment individually, just as you would if you had done this manually. But now you can jump straight to review and applying judgment.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">So we just saw how Agentive used AI to help you automate your expense testing. Let's review that really quickly. We had a plan where we wanted to test accuracy, classification, and cutoff for each one of these 10 selections. We input the selections, uploaded the invoices and bank statements as if we were a client, and then the workflow automatically ran and extracted, based on the GL account details for each selection, what it thought the expenses were. It didn't capture some because it wasn't immediately clear to it, so then we went and gathered that information ourselves using our citations tool, or you can manually type it in. Then we recorded our judgment and reviewed the details.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">With the testing attributes, we were able to click these, look at them, and validate whether the testing attributes, based on the reasoning from AI, were done correctly or incorrectly. These expenses, for example, as a reminder, were categorized as software expenses, but really they were more like training expenses. So this was categorized incorrectly, and we were able to capture our judgment as an exception here and quickly export this to Excel for documenting your workpaper for your audit.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">So what this does for auditors is drastically speed up the execution time of an audit procedure. It helps them jump straight to review. And it's really interesting because we have a client who ran this workpaper on past years' evidence, and they found quite a few mistakes. So this helps improve quality and efficiency, and it helps auditors remember exactly where they left off in applying judgment. Now that we've seen expense testing, we're going to move on to the financial statement review, where we can help you make sure that you have your presentation and accuracy needs of a financial statement mapped. I'll see you there.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\"><br></p></div>   <div style=\"font-size: 14px; margin-top: 20px; text-align: center; color: #ffffff; opacity: 0.8; width: 100%;\"> <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; gap: 0; text-align: center; margin: 20px 0;\">   <hr style=\"flex: 1; border: 0; height: 1px; background: #ffffff; width: calc(100% - 150px);\" />   <img src=\"https://storage.googleapis.com/masterclass-bucket-usa2/media/web-app/miles-masterclass.logo.webp\" alt=\"Miles Masterclass Logo\" style=\"width: 150px; height: auto; margin: 0;\" /> </div></div> </div>",
