        "horizontal_thumbnail_url": "https://milesone-backend-assets.s3.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/MilesOne/Masterclass/Chapter_Thumbnails/Horizontal/agentive5.jpg",
        "vertical_thumbnail_url": null,
        "square_thumbnail_url": null,
        "hls_video_url": "https://d2eoseju8m2c1y.cloudfront.net/videos/Daniel_Alberson_Agentive_Audit_Automation_5_Financial_Statement_Review_in_Agentive_Daniel_Alberson/Daniel_Alberson_Agentive_Audit_Automation_5_Financial_Statement_Review_in_Agentive_Daniel_Alberson.m3u8",
        "transcript_text": "<div style=\"font-family: Arial, sans-serif; background-color: #0d1a2d; color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; padding: 20px; margin: 10px auto; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); max-width: 800px; line-height: 1.6;\">         <div style=\"margin: 15px 0; color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6;\"><h3 style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 24px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.2;;\"><strong style=\"color: #ffffff;;\">Episode 5 - Financial Statement Review in Agentive</strong></h3><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Welcome back. We've looked at revenue, we've looked at expenses. Now let's talk about how we can make sure the most important part of your audit is error-free: the audited financial statements. So we're looking at Agentive now. Let me show you how to do a financial statement review in Agentive when you're inside of an engagement, in this case, our demo client nonprofit audit from 2024.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">I'm going to now click Financial Review. First of all, I just want to say I'm a big REI fan and a loyal customer, which is what led me to testing their financials. So if I click Create here, the first thing that's going to happen is it's going to ask me if we have prior-year financials for comparative statements, which we do. So I'm going to click Continue, and now I'm going to upload those files. I'm going to take the prior-year financial statement, and that's uploaded. Now I'm going to take the current-year financial statement, and I'm going to upload those, and it's going to get started processing.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">I ran this a second ago, so I'm just going to open up the one that I previously ran and show you what that looks like. So that's here, Task Number 37. When I open this up, it's going to take me through the prior-year consistency review, internal consistency review, and footing and cross-footing work for mathematical accuracy. You'll see where we have a review checklist that's coming out shortly. We'd love to show you how we can simplify reviews as well, but let's focus on prior-year consistency first.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">You'll notice that these are all suggested. We can apply all of these and have them marked as confirmed, but we separate everything out by what our AI recommends. So inside each financial statement, we looked at every numeric value, what column header it has, what row heading it has, and what financial statement it's in. We mapped all of this out to compare everything against each other, including the notes to the financials and every part of the document. We're looking at every single value to make sure it matches.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">So here we have REI's audited financials. Our financial statement review suite has already run. We have prior-year consistency, internal consistency, and footing and cross-footing ready to review. So for prior-year consistency, we're going to come and look at what needs review. We have all these items suggested as either consistent or needing review. Coming to Needs Review, we're going to look at this reasoning. It says labels match, but values differ. So inventories for December 2023, if you look here, it ends in 942. And this is in the current-year financial statements. But in the prior year, if we look at the same number, which is labeled as inventories in current assets, it ends in 941. And so this is a simple off-by-one rounding error that seems to be mismatched between these financials. Not a huge deal, but something that is clearly different if someone were comparing these two financials themselves, and it's hard to catch as an individual.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Coming to internal consistency, we're going to see a similar error here where the memberships number is labeled as $465,939, and then separately in the same document, when we look at the consolidated statement of members' equity, that same value is now rounded up by one to $465,940. Now, that may not be a huge discrepancy, but it is a discrepancy that people might pick up on, and it could hurt the reputation of your firm. It's the kind of error we look for when we want to publish structured financials that represent the quality of your work.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Now, coming to footing and cross-footing, we can see a separate set of errors where we have this value here, $1,123,084 in thousands. If you add these up, it actually comes out ending in 85, but the stated result ends in 84. Once again, this is probably a rounding error that happens when you're translating from Excel to Microsoft Word. There have been who knows how many layers of review on this, and this wasn't caught. It's probably been through seven rounds of review and wasn't caught, but our AI did it in a matter of minutes. It takes about five minutes to run because we look at every number individually, what title it has for its row, what column header it has, and what financial statement it's in. So we found this in minutes, and it was missed by countless levels of partner review at Deloitte, which is a great firm. But these mistakes happen, and AI can help us catch them quickly.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Now, let's look at the other issues that happen here. So this one's very easy, right? What's three plus six? Nine. What number do we have here? This is, once again, that memberships value. And so this is where that rounding error comes from. It's ending in 40, and we can clearly see this math doesn't add up. We recognize all dashes as zeros and give you this calculator to quickly review the math that actually happens, and we can see the reasoning explaining the titles of each one of these values.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Coming to the next error, when we take this value and it doesn't map out, actually these total members' equity numbers all added together are off by one as well. And so, once again, not a huge issue, but these small things collectively can undermine the quality of the great work that you've done. Little mistakes like this will slip through humans, but with AI, we can catch them and help you produce higher-quality work for your clients, and it can be done in minutes. So let AI help you do your best work and enjoy your relationship with your clients even more.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">We would love to hear what questions you have, and we'd love to have you try these on your own financial statements to see how you can strengthen the ultimate result of all of your audit work. If you're an auditor who wants AI to be a central part of what you do, not a side experiment, this is the masterclass you can't afford to miss. I quit the stress, I quit the noise, I quit the busy work, I quit the version of audit that drains people, and I chose the version that lets you breathe and still raise the standard.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">So in our tool, we can actually add manual footing and cross-footing and other tick marks with comments and really strong collaboration. So if our AI, for some reason, missed something, you can come here and actually very easily add it. So now we have our manual footing, and if I were to keep going, it's clearly showing that I'm looking for these numbers, and these numbers don't actually match. But I can quickly add manual footing and cross-footing. I can do the same thing for adding internal consistency or prior-year tick marks. You can print these to PDF, review them manually, and it's really easy to collaborate.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">I'm going to just close this out, but I can also take tick marks and even add comments very quickly to let a manager know what I found. Like, actually, let's do it over here. Please note why it's off by one. It may be an Excel rounding issue. We comment this, and the other people on this engagement will get this note sent to their email in batched email updates every four hours or so. So we make it super easy to collaborate, super easy to review financials, and as you make edits in your original PDFs, just upload them. We'll check it all again and help you continue with your progress.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">So thanks for doing this with us. We'd love to help you and your firm and hear how we can continue to simplify the work you do. Thanks.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\"><br></p></div>   <div style=\"font-size: 14px; margin-top: 20px; text-align: center; color: #ffffff; opacity: 0.8; width: 100%;\"> <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; gap: 0; text-align: center; margin: 20px 0;\">   <hr style=\"flex: 1; border: 0; height: 1px; background: #ffffff; width: calc(100% - 150px);\" />   <img src=\"https://storage.googleapis.com/masterclass-bucket-usa2/media/web-app/miles-masterclass.logo.webp\" alt=\"Miles Masterclass Logo\" style=\"width: 150px; height: auto; margin: 0;\" /> </div></div> </div>",
