        "hls_video_url": "https://d2eoseju8m2c1y.cloudfront.net/videos/Daniel_Alberson_Agentive_Audit_Automation_6_Why_AI_Native_Audit_Tools_Win_Daniel_Alberson/Daniel_Alberson_Agentive_Audit_Automation_6_Why_AI_Native_Audit_Tools_Win_Daniel_Alberson.m3u8",
        "transcript_text": "<div style=\"font-family: Arial, sans-serif; background-color: #0d1a2d; color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; padding: 20px; margin: 10px auto; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); max-width: 800px; line-height: 1.6;\">         <div style=\"margin: 15px 0; color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6;\"><h3 style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 24px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.2;;\"><strong style=\"color: #ffffff;;\">Episode 6 - Why AI-Native Audit Tools Win</strong></h3><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Hey, hope y'all enjoyed the demos. What normally happens after we show a demo is we hear questions like, \"This is cool, but I'll wait until my current vendor rolls out the same thing.\" I hear it all the time, and I get it. Switching costs are real. You're invested in your current stack. You have relationships with your vendor, and you're thinking, if AI is this important, won't everyone have it soon? But here's my respectful and honest take.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">I have genuine respect for incumbents in the space. They built platforms that serve hundreds of thousands of auditors. That's not trivial. But here's the architectural reality. Retrofitting AI into a system that was designed 10 or 15 years ago is really, really hard. Not because the people aren't smart. They are. But because the data models, the workflows, and the user interfaces weren't built for this. They were built for a world where the human does all of the work and the software stores the results. To use AI well, to use it the way you just saw, you almost have to design from scratch the way data flows, the way documents are ingested, the way procedures are structured, and the way citations work. All of that has to be AI-native to the architecture.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">And big platforms have a different job than startups. They maintain dozens of products. They protect distribution. They prioritize stability and backward compatibility. They move carefully because they have to. Startups have one job: solve the unsolved problem. That's it. Every engineer, every designer, every dollar points at one problem.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">So here's my honest prediction, and I'll say it plainly. The incumbents will figure this out. They will. But they'll figure it out from watching what we do. They'll learn from it. They'll build their own version, probably two to three years from now. But the pain is now, and you're experiencing it now, and we can help you today. Busy season is coming up. The retention crisis is now. Your clients are impatient. And the generation entering audit right now grew up with AI. They use it every day. They're not going to tolerate 2014-era workflows forever. They'll leave for firms that have better tools, or they'll leave the profession entirely.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">So the question isn't whether this technology is coming to audit. It's whether you want to be ahead of it or behind it. Let me close with something personal. When I transitioned from audit at EY to consulting, I wasn't leaving because I thought audit was beneath me. I was leaving because I believed auditors deserve better, and I didn't know how to build it yet. When I left Meta, I wasn't leaving because tech wasn't exciting. I was leaving because I realized the problems I cared about most, the ones that had been nagging me since my first year as an audit staff, were finally solvable.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">I don't want fewer auditors. I want auditors who love auditing. I want auditing to be more profitable, and I want clients who don't dread the audit every year. And I believe we get there by doing something very specific: keep professional judgment human, automate procedural execution, increase coverage, improve the client experience, and make the work feel modern, because it should be. And we're not guessing here. We're seeing it. BryMar, as you saw in the demos, went from eight hours in one test to an hour and a half. Larson sees a 75% reduction in insurance claim testing procedures. StambaughNess went from 10 hours to two hours in auditing government contract compliance. And consistently across firms, we see about 30% less time spent testing, and this number is going up.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">If you want to see how this can work for your firm, here's the simple next step. Book a demo. Reach out to us. Bring a use case that you feel may be a great fit for automation, one that takes a long time, your auditors don't enjoy, but is really important to your audit quality. Whether it's revenue, expenses, controls, or compliance, whatever keeps your team up at night, we'll help you set up an agent workflow you can reuse.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">And the question we'll answer together is simple: Can we simplify auditing without compromising quality? If the answer is yes, and in our experience it will be, you'll feel it fast: faster testing, better documentation, happier clients, happier auditors. And auditors will have time to actually think, which is what you pay them for.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\">Book a demo at <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" href=\"http://goagentive.com\">goagentive.com</a>, email me at <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" href=\"mailto:danny@goagentive.com\">danny@goagentive.com</a>, or connect with me on LinkedIn. Thanks for spending this hour with me. I really appreciate it. I'm Danny Alberson, and this was my Miles Masterclass, and I hope to see you soon.</p><p style=\"color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px 0; line-height: 1.6;;\"><br></p></div>   <div style=\"font-size: 14px; margin-top: 20px; text-align: center; color: #ffffff; opacity: 0.8; width: 100%;\"> <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; gap: 0; text-align: center; margin: 20px 0;\">   <hr style=\"flex: 1; border: 0; height: 1px; background: #ffffff; width: calc(100% - 150px);\" />   <img src=\"https://storage.googleapis.com/masterclass-bucket-usa2/media/web-app/miles-masterclass.logo.webp\" alt=\"Miles Masterclass Logo\" style=\"width: 150px; height: auto; margin: 0;\" /> </div></div> </div>",
