          "id": "6caff0de-056a-458d-b574-22b7ebb3df42",
          "slug": "ai-agents-and-the-law-who-is-responsible-when-the-machine-acts",
          "title": "AI Agents and the Law: Who is Responsible When the Machine Acts?",
          "short_description": "Explore who is responsible when AI acts, from agency law and liability gaps to court sanctions and governance practices.",
          "description": "AI Agents and the Law: Who Is Responsible When the Machine Acts? with Eran Kahana explores one of the most pressing legal questions of the AI era: how existing legal frameworks apply when autonomous AI systems make decisions and take actions without direct human involvement. Beginning with the fundamental distinction between the computer science concept of an AI agent and the legal concept of agency, the session examines how traditional doctrines of consent, control, fiduciary duty, and authority are being tested by increasingly autonomous technologies. It also explains how laws such as the Uniform Electronic Transactions Act (UETA) and E-SIGN already attribute legal consequences to actions performed by electronic agents, creating significant responsibilities for organizations that deploy them.\r\n\r\nThe session then examines the growing challenges AI presents to established legal principles, including liability, causation, foreseeability, professional responsibility, and ethical practice. Through real-world court decisions, regulatory developments, and practical examples, Eran Kahana demonstrates how AI-generated errors, opaque decision-making, and autonomous actions are reshaping litigation, compliance, and legal risk. Participants will gain a clear understanding of why existing legal obligations remain firmly with human professionals, even when AI systems perform much of the work.\r\n\r\nRather than focusing solely on the risks, the session concludes with practical strategies for responsible AI governance. Eran Kahana introduces actionable frameworks for evaluating AI tools, conducting due diligence, establishing oversight procedures, protecting client data, and building AI literacy across legal organizations. The result is a balanced roadmap that helps legal professionals confidently adopt AI while meeting their ethical duties, managing organizational risk, and maintaining accountability in an increasingly autonomous legal landscape.",
